<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Burgtheater</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1741</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Burgtheater is the national <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, founded in 1741 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> beside the imperial palace and made a court and national theatre by Joseph II in 1776. It staged the plays of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-grillparzer">Franz Grillparzer</a>, and in 1888 it moved to a new building on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ringstrasse">Ringstrasse</a>. It remains one of the leading German-language stages.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/burgtheater.jpg" alt="Burgtheater" width="320" height="181" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: C.Stadler/Bwag · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wien_-_Burgtheater.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
